The HTML parser: a printable ASCII character of UTF-8 input that isn't converted to Unicode (most of a page) goes
straight to the parser's states. It went through HTDecodeUTF8 (a call) and a chain of character-set tests that all
end in keeping the octet as it is: ~40 of the ~150 instructions per character. Wikipedia's article on the 80386
(424 KB): 172 -> 155 M instructions for lynx -dump; the output is the same. With 0007, on the Tab5: that page
18.6 -> 16.7 s (4 runs each, saved page, no network), lite.cnn.com 8.1 -> 7.3, Hacker News 3.6 -> 3.4.

--- a/WWW/Library/Implementation/SGML.c
+++ b/WWW/Library/Implementation/SGML.c
@@ -1700,6 +1700,21 @@
     c = UCH(c_in);
     clong = UCH(c);
 
+    /*
+     * A printable ASCII character of UTF-8 input that isn't converted to
+     * Unicode (most of a page): what HTDecodeUTF8 and the conversions below do
+     * for it (end any multibyte character, keep the octet), at once.
+     */
+    if (me->T.decode_utf8 && c >= ' ' && c < 127 && !me->T.trans_to_uni &&
+	!(UTF8_TTY_ISO2022JP && (me->state == S_nonascii_text
+				 || me->state == S_nonascii_text_sq
+				 || me->state == S_nonascii_text_dq))) {
+	me->U.utf_count = 0;
+	me->U.utf_buf_p = me->U.utf_buf;
+	*(me->U.utf_buf_p) = '\0';
+	goto top1;
+    }
+
     if (me->T.decode_utf8) {
 	switch (HTDecodeUTF8(&(me->U), &c_in, &clong)) {
 	case dUTF8_ok:
